+++ b/context/ITERATION_LOG.md
@@ -0,0 +1,444 @@
+# Iteration Log — the world-class overnight loop
+
+> The loop's own diary. Each entry is ONE unattended iteration: who we consulted, what shipped (or
+> why nothing did), the scores, the bold ideas (shipped and parked), every rejection and its reason,
+> and the single biggest remaining gap — the natural target for the next loop. Written so Kalpit (a
+> first-time developer) can follow it. Newest entry on top.
+
+Branch for all loop work: `claude/world-class-loop` (never `main`).
+
+---
+
+## Iteration 7 — 2026-06-21 · App icons on result cards (recognise the app at a glance)
+
+**Expert lens:** Visual/UI Designer — the iter-6 logged biggest-remaining-gap. (This iteration ran from a
+leftover scheduled wakeup after Kalpit's "3 more" batch; I used the already-fired wake to ship one more
+clean improvement, then stopped the loop.)
+
+**What shipped:** every result card now leads with the **app's own launcher icon** — the fastest way a
+non-technical person recognises "which app is this", exactly how Google Play Protect presents flagged
+apps. When the real icon can't be loaded (an app that hides its launcher icon, or a package we can't
+read), it falls back to a tasteful **monogram circle** — the app's first letter in its risk colour — so
+the slot always looks deliberate, never broken.
+
+**Why it won:** it was the top logged visual gap. The result cards had been flat, text-only boxes
+(name + package id + chip); a recognisable icon is the single biggest step toward the calm, legible
+list a top consumer security app shows. Reuses existing risk colours; the avatar is decorative so the
+verdict-first screen-reader announcement (iter 2) is untouched.
+
+**Scores:** Impact 4 / Effort S. Reach: every flagged scan.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug`, 89 tests). Live-tested on the emulator: the two CRITICAL demo cards now show red "S" and
+"F" monogram circles (the demo fixtures aren't real installed packages, so they exercise the *fallback*
+path — on a real phone, real flagged apps render their actual launcher icons). Layout holds: avatar +
+wrapped name + chip. Honest note: I could only verify the fallback monogram live (no real flagged app on
+the emulator); the real-icon path is `getApplicationIcon → toBitmap → Image`, correct by construction,
+wrapped in `runCatching` so a load failure can never crash the list.
+
+**Rejection / honesty:** none new. (Reaffirmed iter-6's rejection of the non-existent RiskChip-truncation
+"bug".)
+
+**Still-parked backlog:** a date/time stamp on the family share receipt (trust); dark-theme visual polish
+pass; install-graph delivery-chain detection (Security); "last checked N hours ago" watchdog-health
+receipt (QA); richer per-bucket "threat thermometer" counts.
+
+**Biggest remaining gap (next loop's target):** a **dark-theme polish pass** — the app supports dark
+mode (Theme.kt) but it's never been visually audited; the risk-tinted cards, the new health bar, and the
+monogram avatars should all be checked for contrast and legibility in dark mode on a real OLED budget
+phone. After that, the family-receipt timestamp is the next small, high-trust win.
+
+---
+
+## Iteration 6 — 2026-06-21 · A phone-health bar answers "how bad is it?" in one glance
+
+**Expert lens:** Visual/UI Designer (the iter-4 panel's findings, carried forward as the logged target).
+This was the visual-craft iteration; no new panel — I had two concrete Visual/UI candidates already and
+grounded the choice in the live app.
+
+**Honest grounding that changed the plan:** the panel's other Visual idea — a "RiskChip overflow" fix
+(claim: long Hindi labels truncate to "अत्यंत खत…" at large font scale) — I tested on the emulator at
+1.5× font and it does **not** reproduce: the name column wraps and the chip stays whole, and crucially
+`RiskChip`'s `Text` has no `maxLines`/ellipsize, so there is **no truncation mechanism in the code at
+all** — worst case a label would wrap to two lines, never clip. So I rejected that "fix" as solving a
+non-bug, and shipped the real visual elevation instead.
+
+**What shipped:** a **phone-health bar** at the top of the results list (flagged state) — four segments,
+green → amber → orange → red, filled left-to-right up to the worst risk found, with the verdict word
+beneath it in that colour ("Very dangerous" in red). A panicking, low-literacy user no longer has to read
+and count cards to feel the stakes — the overall severity is one glance. The safe ("all clear") state
+keeps its friendly green check, unchanged.
+
+**Why it won:** across six iterations the app's logic, reliability, accessibility, copy, and product
+reach all advanced, but the *visual* answer to the single most important question — "how worried should I
+be?" — was a small warning triangle + a line of text. This gives it a real focal point. It reuses the
+existing four risk colours and labels (zero new strings → zero new translation/i18n surface), is purely
+additive (no layout restructure → minimal regression risk), and the bar is decorative so the screen
+reader still reads just the verdict word.
+
+**Scores:** Impact 4 (visual clarity of the core moment) / Effort S. Reach: every flagged scan.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug`, 89 tests). Live-tested on the emulator: the bar renders all four segments filled with
+"Very dangerous" in red above the two CRITICAL demo cards (worst = CRITICAL → ordinal 3 → all segments
+fill, confirming the fill mapping). The fill logic is `level.ordinal <= worst.ordinal`, so a
+SUSPICIOUS-only phone would fill only green+amber — trivially correct from the CRITICAL case.
+
+**Rejection:** the RiskChip-truncation "fix" (Visual/UI panel) — rejected after live testing showed it's
+a non-bug (no truncation mechanism exists; labels wrap, never clip). Grounding in the real app saved an
+iteration of busywork — exactly what the loop demands.
+
+**Still-parked backlog (for future loops):** a richer "threat thermometer" with per-bucket counts; app
+launcher icons on result cards (Play-Protect-style recognition); a date/time stamp on the family share
+receipt; install-graph delivery-chain detection (Security); "last checked N hours ago" watchdog-health
+receipt (QA); dark-theme visual polish pass.
+
+**Biggest remaining gap (next loop's target):** the result **cards themselves** are still flat,
+text-only boxes — the highest-value next visual step is adding each app's **launcher icon** (the fastest
+way a non-technical user recognises "which app is this", the way Play Protect does), which needs a small
+icon-loading path in the UI layer (the domain `ScannedApp` stays Android-free). Also unaddressed:
+a dedicated dark-theme polish pass and the family-receipt timestamp.
