+## Iteration 5 — 2026-06-21 · "Send result to family" — the remote protector can finally see
+
+**Expert who raised it:** Product Strategist (iter-4 panel), carried forward as iter-4's logged
+biggest-remaining-gap. No new panel this iteration — the target was already chosen and validated; I went
+straight to a clean implementation.
+
+**What shipped:** A **"Send result to family"** button on the scan-results screen (shown whether the
+phone is clean or has threats). It builds a plain-language summary of the scan and opens the phone's own
+share sheet (WhatsApp / SMS / anything) so the worried adult child who set Saavdhan up — often in another
+city — can finally see what a scan found. The actual message (verified live):
+
+> Saavdhan checked this phone for scam apps.
+> These apps need attention:
+> • System Update — Very dangerous
+> • Fast Cash Loan — Very dangerous
+> Please check on whoever uses this phone, and help them remove these.
+> Sent from Saavdhan, an offline anti-scam app.
+
+For a clean phone it instead reads "Good news — no dangerous apps were found." (the reassurance message a
+child can ask a parent to send on demand). Bilingual.
+
+**Why this matters / why it won:** it closes the product's deepest *structural* gap — the unit of
+protection is a relationship (installer ≠ victim), but until now nothing Saavdhan produced ever reached
+the person who could actually act. And it does so **without breaking the no-internet promise**: it uses a
+standard Android `ACTION_SEND` share sheet, so Saavdhan itself sends nothing over any network, stores no
+contact, and adds no permission ([ADR-0014](../docs/decisions/0014-offline-share-to-family.md)). A
+conventional team would have reached for accounts + a caregiver dashboard + cloud sync — all forbidden
+here; the bold move was to realise WhatsApp is already the family's trusted channel and the OS share sheet
+is the whole delivery mechanism.
+
+**Scores:** Impact 5 / Effort S–M. Reach: every family that uses the app — turns a single-device tool into
+a two-person safety net.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug :app:lintDebug`)
+with **3 new unit tests** (suite 86 → 89) on the pure `buildFamilyReport` assembler — clean vs flagged
+state, never mixing the safe line with the threat advice, intro-leads/footer-trails. **Live-tested on the
+emulator end-to-end:** tapped the button, the OS share sheet opened, and the dumped share text was exactly
+the receipt above (both apps, correct levels, advice line present because threats exist). Privacy surface
+is zero — no recipient stored, the user picks who to send to each time.
+
+**Notes for the next loop:**
+- The receipt has no timestamp/date yet (kept it simple). Adding "checked on 21 June, 9:40 PM" would raise
+  trust — small backlog item.
+- A physical Android phone got plugged into this machine mid-iteration, so adb now sees two devices; all
+  emulator commands must use `-s emulator-5554` (never touch the real device).
+
+**Still-parked backlog:** the "threat thermometer" results header (Visual/UI, Impact 5 / Effort M); the
+RiskChip overflow-robustness fix (Visual/UI, Impact 4 / Effort S); install-graph delivery-chain detection
+(Security); "last checked N hours ago" watchdog-health receipt (QA).
+
+**Biggest remaining gap (next iteration's target):** a real **Visual/UI Designer** pass. Across six
+iterations the app's logic, reliability, accessibility, copy, and product reach are now strong, but the
+*visual* craft is still default-Material-3 plain — the result cards, risk chips, and safe-state are
+functional but not yet beautiful or distinctive. The "threat thermometer" header or the chip-robustness
+fix are the concrete shippable slices.
