+## Iteration 4 — 2026-06-21 · The mid-scam alert now breaks the scammer's spell
+
+**Experts consulted (3 independent parallel Sonnet subagents — rotated to the untouched lenses):**
+Visual/UI Designer · UX Writer · Product Strategist. (All ten roster lenses have now been consulted
+at least once across the night.)
+
+**What shipped:** New wording for the background "watchdog" alert — the notification that pops up the
+instant a dangerous app is installed, which is usually the exact moment a scammer is on the phone
+walking the victim through it. The old text described the artifact ("A new app looks dangerous · X may
+be a scam app. Tap to check it in Saavdhan.") — calm, reflective language for someone who isn't in a
+crisis. The new text speaks to the person actually mid-manipulation and leads with the one thing that
+can break the spell:
+
+- New install — title: "Is someone on the phone telling you to do this?" · body: "If yes, hang up now
+  and do not share any OTP. %1$s was just installed and looks dangerous. Tap to see what to do."
+- Escalation (an existing app just gained dangerous powers — the real SpyNote "enable Accessibility
+  days later" trick) — title: "Did someone just ask you to turn on a setting?" · body: "If yes, stop
+  and do not share any OTP. %1$s now has powers that scam apps misuse. Tap to see what to do."
+
+Both mirrored in Hindi. The instruction ("hang up, don't share an OTP") works even if the user never
+taps — the notification's job is to interrupt the call, not to drive a product flow.
+
+**Why it won (over the Visual/UI and Product ideas):** it's the highest impact-per-effort change in the
+whole app — a pure two-locale string edit, zero code, zero new permissions, lowest possible regression
+risk — landing at the single most valuable second in the product: mid-scam, before any money moves. No
+other string fires at that moment. It was also the most overdue idea (parked since iteration 1).
+
+**The honesty adaptation (important):** the UX Writer's draft flatly asserted "You are being scammed.
+X was installed by a scammer." I deliberately did NOT ship that. The watchdog can fire on a HIGH app the
+user genuinely sideloaded on purpose (e.g. a bank APK from the bank's own site), so a flat accusation
+would sometimes be false — and Saavdhan's core promise is to be an honest detective, never to overclaim
+([ADR-0002](../docs/decisions/0002-detective-not-enforcer.md)). I kept the trance-breaking *question*
+and the concrete safe action, but kept "looks dangerous" instead of "is a scam."
+
+**Scores:** Impact 5 / Effort S. Reach: every user the watchdog ever protects — at the most reversible
+moment of a scam.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug`, 86 tests) — this validates format-string consistency and both-locale mirroring.
+Honest limitation: I could NOT capture the notification live on the emulator, because triggering it
+requires a genuinely new sideloaded dangerous install diffed against the watchdog's saved baseline
+(the demo fixtures are deliberately excluded from the watchdog), which isn't stageable in this loop. The
+change is pure copy, reviewed in both locales; `ThreatNotifier.kt` already passes the app label into
+these exact keys, so no code path changed.
+
+**Bold ideas raised this panel (backlog — the slate for the next iterations):**
+1. **"Send to family" offline share receipt (Product Strategist, Impact 5 / Effort S–M).** The real unit
+   of protection is the relationship: the adult child who installs Saavdhan on a parent's phone, often
+   remote. Add a "Share result" button that opens the standard Android share sheet with a plain-language,
+   pre-written summary ("Saavdhan checked this phone… found 1 dangerous app: System Update…") to send via
+   the family's existing WhatsApp/SMS. Fully offline (`ACTION_SEND`, no network, no account). → strong
+   candidate for the next iteration.
+2. **"Threat thermometer" results header (Visual/UI, Impact 5 / Effort M).** A sticky 4-segment
+   green→red bar answering "how bad is my phone overall?" in one glance, above the card list.
+3. **RiskChip overflow robustness (Visual/UI, Impact 4 / Effort S).** The "Very dangerous" pill sits in a
+   `SpaceBetween` row with no width floor on the name column; long Hindi labels at 1.5× font scale can
+   collide/truncate on a 360dp budget screen. Cheap, real robustness fix.
+
+**Rejections:** none outright this iteration — the two non-chosen findings are strong and parked as the
+backlog above. (One sub-claim rejected: the UX Writer's "you ARE being scammed" certainty — see honesty
+note.)
+
+**Biggest remaining gap (next iteration's target):** the **"Send to family" share receipt** — it closes
+the product's deepest structural gap (the remote protector can't see or act on what the local victim's
+phone found) while staying 100% offline. The Visual/UI robustness + thermometer are the loop after.
