+## Iteration 3 — 2026-06-21 · The scan no longer silently fails on cheap phones (Binder limit)
+
+**Expert consulted (1 focused Android Engineer subagent):** rotated to the last untouched core
+engineering lens. (Consulted so far: Detection/Rule-Engine, UX Writer, Interaction Designer [iter 1];
+Accessibility, Security, QA [iter 2]; Android Engineer [iter 3]. Still untouched: Visual/UI, Product
+Strategist, UX Researcher.) This iteration's target was the #1 gap I logged last loop and deliberately
+deferred — so I ran one sharp design-review subagent to pressure-test the fix rather than a fresh
+discovery panel.
+
+**What shipped:** The app scan used to read every installed app in ONE big system call loaded with
+heavy data. On a cheap phone with hundreds of apps (TECNO/itel/Infinix/bloated Xiaomi — exactly
+Saavdhan's users), that one call can blow past Android's ~1 MB "Binder" reply limit and **fail the
+whole scan silently** — either by crashing (you'd see an error screen, and the background watchdog
+would retry forever and stop alerting) or, on some Xiaomi/Oppo ROMs, by quietly returning a SHORTER
+list with no error (the worst case — a scam app could hide in the missing tail and never be checked).
+
+Now the scan is resilient: it takes a cheap app-count first, tries the fast bulk call, and trusts it
+only if it neither failed nor came back suspiciously short (< 95% of the count). Otherwise it falls
+back to reading each app one-by-one (which can't hit the limit), skips any that fail, and honestly
+marks the scan "incomplete" instead of pretending all-clear. The background watchdog also got a retry
+cap so a persistent failure can't become an endless silent retry-storm.
+
+**Why it won:** it's the most dangerous *real* bug in the app — a silent failure to protect, on the
+exact hardware our users own — and this iteration I could fix it *safely and verifiably* because I
+built a small Android-free, unit-testable seam for the decision logic (the reason it was deferred in
+iter 2 was the lack of such a seam).
+
+**Scores:** Impact 5 (it's the safety promise failing silently) / Effort S–M. Reach: every user on a
+budget phone with many apps — a large slice of the target base.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug`) with **6 new unit tests** (suite 80 → 86) proving every branch — fast path, throw →
+fallback, silent-truncation → fallback, partial flag, install-race tolerance, names-call failure.
+Live-tested on the emulator: a normal scan still finds and rates both demo apps correctly with **no
+spurious "incomplete" note** (the fast path is byte-for-byte unregressed when counts match). The
+fallback can't be reproduced on the emulator (can't force the Binder crash), which is exactly why it's
+covered by deterministic unit tests instead. Recorded as
+[ADR-0013](../docs/decisions/0013-resilient-package-fetch.md).
+
+**How the expert review sharpened the fix (cross-pollination):** the Android Engineer caught two things
+my first design missed — (1) the **silent-truncation** failure mode (not just the throw), which is why
+the fix compares counts rather than only catching exceptions; and (2) the fallback loop must catch
+broadly, not rely on the existing `NameNotFoundException`-only handler. I kept the generic testable
+seam *against* his "over-engineered" note, because his simpler alternative (test with `T=PackageInfo`)
+literally can't run off-device — the generic seam is what makes pure-JVM testing possible, which was
+the whole mandate.
+
+**Bold ideas raised (backlog — not shipped):**
+- **Parallelise the fallback per-app fetches** (`async`/`awaitAll`, bounded concurrency) to cut the
+  slow-path latency on bloated phones. Optimisation, not correctness — parked.
+- Still parked from earlier loops: install-graph "scam delivery chain" detection (Security, iter 2);
+  the "last checked N hours ago" watchdog-health receipt (QA, iter 2); the mid-scam attacker-script
+  watchdog copy reframe (UX Writer, iter 1).
+
+**Rejections (with reasons):**
+- The Android Engineer's "drop the generic seam, it's over-engineered" — **rejected**: his simpler
+  inline alternative can't be unit-tested without Robolectric (you can't construct `PackageInfo`
+  off-device), and a JVM-testable seam was the explicit requirement. Kept it, single type parameter,
+  minimal.
+- A redundant `Dispatchers.Default → Dispatchers.IO` hop in `ScanViewModel` he flagged — real but
+  trivial and out of scope; left for a future cleanup loop to avoid scope creep.
+
+**Biggest remaining gap to world-class (next loop's target):** the app has now had three engineering/
+reliability/accessibility passes but **no Visual/UI or Product-Strategy lens yet**. The strongest
+untouched direction is a **Visual/UI Designer** pass — the result cards, risk chips, and detail screen
+are functional but plain (flat cards, one accent colour per level); a best-in-class visual polish pass
+(spacing rhythm, type scale, chip treatment, empty/safe-state delight) judged against a top consumer
+security app could lift trust for non-technical users. Alternatively, the parked **mid-scam watchdog
+copy reframe** remains the highest-leverage single-moment UX win. Pick one next.
+
+---
