+## Iteration 2 — 2026-06-21 · Screen reader hears the verdict first, not the package id
+
+**Experts consulted (3 independent parallel Sonnet subagents — rotated to overdue lenses):**
+Accessibility Specialist · Security Engineer · QA/Reliability Engineer.
+(Consulted so far: Detection/Rule-Engine, UX Writer, Interaction Designer [iter 1]; Accessibility,
+Security, QA [iter 2]. Still untouched: Visual/UI, Product Strategy, UX Research, Android Engineer.)
+
+**What shipped:** A blind or low-vision user (a core part of who this app is for) now hears the danger
+**verdict first**, then the app name — on both the scan-results cards and the app-detail banner. Before
+this, TalkBack read each card as "System Update, com.demo.systemupdate, Very dangerous, double-tap to
+activate" — the alarming word came *last*, and the package id was spelled out as meaningless gibberish.
+Now it's one clean spoken line: **"Very dangerous — System Update, Button"**, and the tap is labelled
+**"See what to do"** instead of the generic "activate". The package id is no longer read aloud anywhere.
+
+How it was done (plain English): each card / banner now overrides its screen-reader description
+(`clearAndSetSemantics`) with one ordered sentence built from the risk label + app name, and the result
+card declares itself a labelled button. The **sighted layout is completely unchanged** — only what the
+screen reader says changed. Two new mirrored strings: `cd_risk_summary` ("%1$s — %2$s") and
+`action_see_details` ("See what to do" / "क्या करें देखें").
+
+**Why this won (over the other two finalists):** it's the one finding that is simultaneously
+high-impact, low-regression, AND fully verifiable inside an unattended loop. It reaches the exact
+vulnerable population the app exists for, on the most-traveled screen, and it closes an item that was
+sitting open on our own accessibility checklist (`docs/12-accessibility.md`: "does the risk level get
+announced with the app name?").
+
+**Scores:** Impact 5 / Effort S. Reach: every screen-reader user, every scan with a result.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green (`ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug`, 80 tests). Live-tested on the emulator by dumping the actual accessibility tree
+(`uiautomator dump`): the result-card node reports `class=android.widget.Button`, `clickable=true`,
+`content-desc="Very dangerous — System Update"`; the detail banner reports one node
+`content-desc="Very dangerous — Fast Cash Loan"`; and `com.demo.systemupdate` appears in **zero**
+spoken nodes. Sighted screenshot confirms the visual card is unchanged. Also documented in
+`docs/12-accessibility.md`.
+
+**Bold ideas raised (backlog — not shipped):**
+1. **Inverted-urgency TalkBack (Accessibility, Impact 5 / Effort S)** — announce the verdict before
+   the name even though the eye reads the name first. ✅ **Effectively shipped this iteration** (the
+   `cd_risk_summary` order is exactly this).
+2. **Install-graph "scam delivery chain" detection (Security, Impact 4 / Effort M)** — read
+   `InstallSourceInfo.originatingPackageName` (API 30+, already permitted) to trace that a malware
+   payload "came via 'Fast Loan APK', installed 10 days ago", and escalate both. Surfaces the human
+   social-engineering layer no point-in-time scanner sees. Parked — genuinely novel and on-mission;
+   needs a careful design (persist a small ancestry map) and its own iteration.
+3. **"Last checked" resurrection receipt (QA, Impact 4 / Effort S)** — show "Saavdhan last checked
+   your phone: 3 hours ago" on the scan screen, amber/red if a gap means an OEM killed the watchdog.
+   Turns invisible background-kill into a visible, actionable signal. Parked — nice, small, worth a
+   future loop.
+
+**Rejections (with reasons):**
+- **Security — `signingCertificateHistory` allowlist bypass (claimed Impact 5):** the agent's
+  attack (forge a cert whose SHA-256 collides with a trusted bank cert in `KnownApps.TRUSTED_SIGNATURES`)
+  is **cryptographically infeasible** — you'd need a bank's private key or a SHA-256 break. Real residual
+  nit: `AppScanner.getSignatureHashes` (line 199) reads `signingCertificateHistory` (full key lineage)
+  for single-signer apps where `apkContentsSigners` (current signer only) would be more correct. Low
+  practical impact → small hardening backlog, not worth this loop.
+- **QA — `TransactionTooLargeException` on the bulk scan (real, Impact 5):** VERIFIED genuine —
+  `AppScanner.installedPackages` (lines 164-175) fetches `GET_PERMISSIONS | GET_SIGNING_CERTIFICATES |
+  GET_ACTIVITIES` for *all* packages in one Binder call, which can exceed the 1 MB limit on cheap
+  phones with many apps and fail the whole scan silently (watchdog retries forever; UI shows Error).
+  **Deferred, not dismissed** — it's Effort M, rewrites the hottest path (regression risk), and
+  **cannot be live-verified in an unattended loop** (the emulator won't reproduce the crash; there is
+  no PackageManager test seam yet). Shipping an unverified change to the core scan path would violate
+  the loop's safety posture. **This is the #1 biggest remaining gap (see below).**
+
+**Biggest remaining gap to world-class (next loop's target):** the **`TransactionTooLargeException`
+two-pass scan + a test seam**. It is arguably the most dangerous real bug in the app — a *silent total
+scan failure on exactly the cheap-phone demographic Saavdhan targets*, which leaves a user unprotected
+with no error they'd notice. It deserves a dedicated iteration: introduce a thin seam over
+`PackageManager` so the scan logic is unit-testable, split the fetch into a light name-pass + per-app
+detail-pass that skips oversized packages and reports `partial=true`, and add a test that injects a
+stub throwing `TransactionTooLargeException`. Also still-unconsulted: Visual/UI, Product Strategy.
