+## Iteration 1 — 2026-06-21 · Rank the danger reasons by the engine's own weights
+
+**Experts consulted (3, run as independent parallel subagents on Sonnet):**
+Detection / Rule-Engine Engineer · UX Writer · Interaction Designer.
+(First loop, so no rotation history yet. Accessibility, Security, QA, Visual/UI, Product Strategy,
+UX Research are all still untouched — candidates for the next loops.)
+
+**What shipped:** On the app-detail screen, the list of reasons under "Why this looks risky" is now
+ranked **most-dangerous-first**, using the very same point-weights the risk engine uses to decide the
+verdict. When an app has both decisive powers (e.g. Accessibility, impersonation) and soft
+circumstantial clues (e.g. "installed recently"), the soft clues are split off into a quieter "Also
+noticed" group below. Before this, the reasons were shown in the random order they were collected —
+which put **impersonation, the single most damning clue, dead last**, beneath weaker reasons.
+
+How it was done (plain English):
+- The engine's per-signal points lived as scattered magic numbers inside one function. They are now
+  one `WEIGHTS` map — a single source of truth — that both the scoring and the UI read. So the score
+  and the explanation can never disagree.
+- The UI sorts the reasons by those weights and tiers them. New strings `detail_also_noticed`
+  (English + Hindi) added and mirrored.
+- Two new unit tests lock it in: every signal must have a weight, and the score must equal the sum of
+  its reasons' weights. (RiskEngine tests: 30 → 32; total suite 78 → 80.)
+
+**Why this won (over the other two finalists):** it closes a real gap between two of the project's
+own core values — the *explainable* engine ([ADR-0009](../docs/decisions/0009-deterministic-rule-engine.md))
+and the plain-language UI — that until now stopped at the data layer. It reaches the highest-traffic
+threat screen (every flagged app's detail), it's deterministic and unit-testable, and it carries
+almost no regression risk (it regroups existing data; no flow change). Recorded as
+[ADR-0012](../docs/decisions/0012-explanation-ranked-by-engine-weight.md).
+
+**Scores:** Impact 5 / Effort S (Detection Engineer's framing). Reach: every CRITICAL/HIGH/SUSPICIOUS
+app a user opens.
+
+**Verification:** Full pre-commit check green — `ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug
+:app:lintDebug` all pass (80 tests). Live-tested on the `saavdhan_pixel` emulator in English:
+- *System Update* (real leftover package, 3 signals): impersonation now leads the list (it used to
+  be last). ✔
+- *Fast Cash Loan* (synthetic demo, 4 signals): leads with Accessibility → SMS → Sideloaded, and the
+  "Also noticed" group correctly shows "Installed very recently" in quieter, greyed text. ✔
+
+**Bold ideas raised (the backlog — not yet shipped):**
+1. **Watchdog notification = the attacker's script (UX Writer, Impact 5 / Effort S).** When a
+   dangerous app is installed mid-scam, the notification currently says "A new app looks dangerous."
+   Reframe it to match what the victim is hearing on the call: *"Did someone on the phone just ask
+   you to install this? If yes, hang up now."* Pure copy change to `watchdog_*` strings (EN + Hindi).
+   Parked, not rejected — strong idea; narrower reach than the detail screen, and the "you're on a
+   call" assumption needs a real-family sanity check first.
+2. **"One threat, one screen" — auto-open the worst offender (Interaction Designer, Impact 5 /
+   Effort S).** Skip the results list and land the panicking user straight on the worst app's detail.
+   **Rejected this iteration:** it rewrites a working navigation flow, hides the full picture, and
+   could disorient someone who scanned to check one *specific* app — too big a bet to land unverified
+   in one unattended loop (risks non-negotiable #5, "no regressions"). Worth a careful, tested
+   revisit — possibly as an *option* rather than the default.
+
+**Other rejection:** none beyond #2 above.
+
+**Housekeeping found along the way (dev-only, not a user bug):** the emulator has a *real* leftover
+`com.demo.systemupdate` package that shadows the synthetic demo fixture (the scan dedupes by package
+name), so that demo shows only its 3 metadata-derivable signals. Harmless to users; only affects what
+the on-emulator demo looks like. Backlog: `adb uninstall com.demo.systemupdate` before demoing, or
+give the fixtures package names that can't collide.
+
+**Biggest remaining gap to world-class (next loop's natural target):** the **watchdog / mid-scam
+moment** (idea #1). The detail screen is now sharp, but the highest-leverage instant — the
+notification that fires *while the scam is happening* — still speaks in technical-artifact language
+("dangerous app detected") instead of intercepting the social-engineering script. That, plus the
+still-unconsulted **Accessibility** and **Security** lenses, are the strongest candidates next.
